 * ---
 *
 * ## Overview
 *
 * PicoKernel is the OS brain. Security-conscious
 * design is a project requirement, not a feature added later.
 * The principles on this page apply throughout the codebase and are
 * enforced at review time without exception.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Memory Safety
 *
 * | Mechanism | Purpose |
 * |-----------|---------|
 * | Canary value `0xDEADFACEDEADFACE` after every allocation | Detects heap corruption, validated on every `k_free()` |
 * | `__FILE__` / `__LINE__` tracking per allocation | Every live block records its call site for debugging |
 * | Explicit panic on invariant violation | No silent undefined behaviour `k_panic()` is always preferred |
 * | `k_dump` behind `#ifdef KERNEL_DEBUG` | Full heap inspection available only in debug builds |
 * | Bounds-check before all pointer arithmetic | No `ptr + offset` without size validation |
 * | No unbounded `memcpy` / `strcpy` | Explicit sizes required, always |
 *
 * ### Canary validation
 *
 * Every block allocated by `k_alloc()` has a canary placed immediately
 * after the usable region. `k_free()` validates the canary before releasing
 * the block. A corrupted canary triggers `k_panic()` immediately the kernel does not attempt to free a corrupted block.
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * #define HEAP_CANARY 0xDEADFACEDEADFACEULL
 *
 * // Validated inside k_free() before any deallocation
 * if (block->canary != HEAP_CANARY)
 * {
 *     k_panic("allocator: heap corruption detected");
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @warning Removing or bypassing canary validation is a review blocker.
 * No exceptions.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Layering as a Security Boundary
 *
 * The strict architectural layering is also a security containment mechanism.
 *
 * | Boundary | What it prevents |
 * |----------|-----------------|
 * | Module → Driver blocked | A compromised module cannot write to hardware directly |
 * | Interface → Driver blocked | An interface bug cannot bypass the scheduler or access devices |
 * | Drivers kept minimal | Less driver code means less attack surface |
 * | Kernel as single choke point | All hardware state changes are auditable in one place |
 *
 * Full layer rules: @ref arch_overview.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Debug Gate Policy
 *
 * All diagnostic instrumentation **must** be gated behind `#ifdef KERNEL_DEBUG`.
 *
 * Release builds must not expose:
 * - Internal heap layout
 * - Register contents
 * - Task state or scheduler internals
 * - Verbose diagnostic output over any channel
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * #ifdef KERNEL_DEBUG
 *     k_dump_heap();
 *     k_dump_task_table();
 * #endif
 * @endcode
 *
 * @warning Submitting unconditionally-enabled debug output is a review blocker.
 * It must be corrected before merge, no exceptions for "temporary" debug prints.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Panic Policy
 *
 * When a kernel invariant is violated: **panic**. Do not attempt to recover
 * from a state the kernel cannot verify.
 *
 * | Rule | Detail |
 * |------|--------|
 * | Panic should include source location | `__FILE__` and `__LINE__` always emitted |
 * | Panic includes a human-readable message | Describes what invariant was violated |
 * | Panics are never swallowed | No `if (panic_condition) { return; }` |
 * | Panic halts execution | No recovery path; hardware reset is the only recovery |
 * | A visible panic is better than silent corruption | - |
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * // Correct - explicit, descriptive panic
 * if (block == NULL)
 * {
 *     k_panic("k_alloc: out of heap memory increase HEAP_SIZE");
 * }
 *
 * // Wrong - silent failure
 * if (block == NULL)
 * {
 *     return NULL;  // caller may dereference NULL
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Defensive Coding Guidelines
 *
 * | Practice | Required |
 * |----------|----------|
 * | Validate all parameters at public API boundaries | ✅ Yes |
 * | Treat all external input as untrusted | ✅ Yes |
 * | Assert preconditions with `k_panic()`, not silent return | ✅ Yes |
 * | Use `_Static_assert` for compile-time invariants | ✅ Recommended |
 * | Mark unreachable paths with `k_panic("unreachable")` | ✅ Yes |
 * | Initialise all variables before use | ✅ Yes |
 * | Zero-initialise security-sensitive buffers after use | ✅ Yes |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Driver Restrictions
 *
 * Drivers are the only layer permitted to access hardware. This restriction
 * is both an architectural rule and a security policy.
 *
 * | Restriction | Enforcement |
 * |-------------|-------------|
 * | No direct hardware register access outside drivers | Review blocker |
 * | No Pico SDK hardware calls outside drivers | Review blocker |
 * | Drivers contain no business logic | Review blocker |
 * | Drivers expose no state to layers above the kernel | Architectural requirement |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Secure Development Checklist
 *
 * Before submitting code that touches the kernel or drivers, verify:
 *
 * - [ ] No allocation without a corresponding bounds check
 * - [ ] No pointer arithmetic without size validation
 * - [ ] All error paths result in explicit handling, panic or documented return code
 * - [ ] No diagnostic output outside `#ifdef KERNEL_DEBUG`
 * - [ ] No direct hardware register access outside drivers
 * - [ ] Canary validation is present and not bypassed
 * - [ ] Security-sensitive buffers are zeroed after use
 * - [ ] All public API parameters are validated at the boundary
 *
